WEBVTT

00:00.487 --> 00:01.320
- [Voiceover] We're told that a particle

00:01.320 --> 00:04.913
moves along the curve x squared, y squared

00:04.913 --> 00:07.321
is equal to 16, so that
the the x-coordinate

00:07.321 --> 00:09.514
is changing at a constant rate

00:09.514 --> 00:12.264
of negative two units per minute.

00:14.008 --> 00:16.683
What is the rate of
change in units per minute

00:16.683 --> 00:19.023
of the particle's y-coordinate

00:19.023 --> 00:22.273
when the particle is at the point 1, 4?

00:23.250 --> 00:26.128
So let's just repeat, or
rewrite, what they told us.

00:26.128 --> 00:28.568
So the curve is described by x squared,

00:28.568 --> 00:30.651
y squared is equal to 16.

00:31.696 --> 00:32.938
They tell us that up there.

00:32.938 --> 00:34.572
They tell us that the x-coordinate

00:34.572 --> 00:37.197
is changing at a constant rate.

00:37.197 --> 00:38.471
We underline that.

00:38.471 --> 00:39.633
The x-coordinate is changing at

00:39.633 --> 00:42.931
a constant rate of negative
two units per minute.

00:42.931 --> 00:45.473
So we could say that dx ...

00:45.473 --> 00:47.291
I'll write over here
on the right hand side.

00:47.291 --> 00:48.707
Dx, dt.

00:48.707 --> 00:50.731
The rate of change of the x-coordinate

00:50.731 --> 00:53.726
with respect to time is
equal to negative two.

00:53.726 --> 00:57.643
And they're saying units,
some unit of distance,

00:58.775 --> 01:01.598
units divided by minute.

01:01.598 --> 01:03.015
Units per minute.

01:04.304 --> 01:05.757
What they want us to figure out

01:05.757 --> 01:07.381
is what is the rate of change

01:07.381 --> 01:09.679
of the particle's y-coordinate.

01:09.679 --> 01:10.963
So let me underline that.

01:10.963 --> 01:15.150
What is the rate of change of
the particle's y-coordinate?

01:15.150 --> 01:19.789
So what they want us to
find is what is dy, dt.

01:19.789 --> 01:21.221
What is that equal to?

01:21.221 --> 01:25.258
And they say when the
particle is at the point 1,4,

01:25.258 --> 01:29.176
so when x is equal to
one, so x is equal to one.

01:29.176 --> 01:33.268
And y is equal to four,
y is equal to four.

01:33.268 --> 01:36.463
So can we set up some
equation that involves

01:36.463 --> 01:38.773
the rate of change of x with respect to t,

01:38.773 --> 01:42.019
y with respect to t, x and y?

01:42.019 --> 01:43.918
Well what if we were
to take the derivative

01:43.918 --> 01:47.019
of this relation that describes the curve?

01:47.019 --> 01:48.170
What if we were to take the derivative

01:48.170 --> 01:50.541
with respect to t on both sides?

01:50.541 --> 01:52.421
So let me write that down.

01:52.421 --> 01:53.500
So we're going to take the derivative.

01:53.500 --> 01:56.083
Actually let me just erase this

01:58.458 --> 02:01.791
so I have a little bit more space.

02:01.791 --> 02:03.105
Alright.

02:03.105 --> 02:06.103
That way I can just add it.

02:06.103 --> 02:09.443
So let's take the derivative with respect

02:09.443 --> 02:11.693
to t on both sides of that.

02:13.122 --> 02:14.384
And if at any point you get inspired

02:14.384 --> 02:16.685
I encourage you to pause the video

02:16.685 --> 02:19.076
and try to work through it.

02:19.076 --> 02:20.625
Well on the left hand side, if we view

02:20.625 --> 02:24.340
this as a product of two
functions right over here,

02:24.340 --> 02:28.507
we could take the derivative
of the first function,

02:30.095 --> 02:32.749
which is going to be the derivative

02:32.749 --> 02:35.571
of x squared with respect to x.

02:35.571 --> 02:39.440
So that is 2x, and remember we're not

02:39.440 --> 02:41.948
just taking the derivative
with respect to x,

02:41.948 --> 02:43.710
we're taking the derivative
with respect to t,

02:43.710 --> 02:44.995
so we're going to have
to apply the chain rule.

02:44.995 --> 02:46.301
So it's going to be the
derivative of x squared

02:46.301 --> 02:48.325
with respect to x, which is 2x,

02:48.325 --> 02:51.490
times the derivative
of x with respect to t.

02:51.490 --> 02:52.823
So times dx, dt.

02:55.021 --> 02:56.118
And then we're going to multiply

02:56.118 --> 02:58.508
that times the second function.

02:58.508 --> 03:00.091
So times y squared.

03:01.068 --> 03:02.580
Times y squared.

03:02.580 --> 03:04.141
And then that's going to be plus

03:04.141 --> 03:08.262
the first function,
which is just x squared

03:08.262 --> 03:10.211
times the derivative of the second

03:10.211 --> 03:12.243
function with respect to d.

03:12.243 --> 03:13.686
So once again we're going
to apply the chain rule.

03:13.686 --> 03:18.233
The derivative of y-squared
with respect to y is 2y.

03:18.233 --> 03:19.849
I'm gonna do that in that orange color.

03:19.849 --> 03:21.349
It is equal to 2y.

03:23.891 --> 03:27.615
And then times the derivative
of y with respect to t.

03:27.615 --> 03:28.698
Times dy, dt.

03:30.790 --> 03:32.985
And then that is going to be equal

03:32.985 --> 03:36.624
to the derivative with respect to t of 16.

03:36.624 --> 03:38.404
Well that doesn't change over time,

03:38.404 --> 03:41.479
so that's just going to be equal to zero.

03:41.479 --> 03:42.564
So here we have it.

03:42.564 --> 03:44.255
We need to simplify this a little bit.

03:44.255 --> 03:46.142
But we have an equation that gives

03:46.142 --> 03:49.084
our relationship between
x, derivative of x

03:49.084 --> 03:52.998
with respect to t, y, and
derivative of y with respect to t.

03:52.998 --> 03:54.220
So actually let me just rewrite

03:54.220 --> 03:56.314
it one more time so it's
a little bit simplified.

03:56.314 --> 03:59.564
So this is 2xy squared dx, dt, plus ...

04:05.032 --> 04:06.702
Actually I don't even
have to rewrite it again.

04:06.702 --> 04:09.762
All we're trying to do
is solve for dy, dt,

04:09.762 --> 04:12.055
so let's actually just
substitute the values in.

04:12.055 --> 04:13.982
So we know we want to figure out

04:13.982 --> 04:16.104
what's going on when x is equal to one.

04:16.104 --> 04:20.137
So we know that the x's
here are equal to one.

04:20.137 --> 04:23.334
This x, x squared, well that's
just going to be one squared,

04:23.334 --> 04:25.273
so that's just going to be equal to one.

04:25.273 --> 04:27.321
We know that y is equal to four.

04:27.321 --> 04:31.488
So this is going to be 16 and
this is going to be eight.

04:33.895 --> 04:35.603
We know the derivative of x with

04:35.603 --> 04:37.217
respect to t is negative two.

04:37.217 --> 04:39.736
They tell us that in
the problem statement.

04:39.736 --> 04:41.000
Negative two.

04:41.000 --> 04:43.895
So now this is a good time
to simplify this thing.

04:43.895 --> 04:46.228
So this will simplify to ...

04:47.091 --> 04:48.940
Let's see, all of this is going

04:48.940 --> 04:50.976
to be two times one times negative two,

04:50.976 --> 04:53.809
so that is negative four times 16.

04:55.704 --> 04:59.371
So that is negative 64,
and then we have ...

05:01.963 --> 05:05.064
We'll use a color you can see.

05:05.064 --> 05:06.516
And then we have all of this.

05:06.516 --> 05:08.930
Well this is just going to be
one time eight times dy, dt.

05:08.930 --> 05:11.426
So this going to be eight dy, dt.

05:11.426 --> 05:14.340
So plus eight times the derivative of y

05:14.340 --> 05:17.084
with respect to t is equal to zero.

05:17.084 --> 05:20.203
Add 64 to both sides and we get ...

05:20.203 --> 05:21.741
I'll switch to a neutral color.

05:21.741 --> 05:23.523
Eight times the derivative of y

05:23.523 --> 05:26.672
with respect to t is equal to 64.

05:26.672 --> 05:30.291
Divide both sides by eight and you get

05:30.291 --> 05:33.298
the derivative of y with respect to t

05:33.298 --> 05:37.480
is equal to 64 divided by eight is just 8.

05:37.480 --> 05:39.726
If you want to look at the units

05:39.726 --> 05:42.456
it will also be in units per minute.

05:42.456 --> 05:45.206
Some units of distance per minute.

05:47.383 --> 05:48.716
And we are done.
